WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.244
An xor filter, also by Graf and Lemire

2
00:00:03.244 --> 00:00:05.660
stores a table of small values.

3
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.113
Every key gets a short fingerprint, f of x

4
00:00:09.113 --> 00:00:10.340
and three slots

5
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.650
one in each third of the table.

6
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.361
Construction must fill the table so

7
00:00:15.428 --> 00:00:17.856
that the values in every key's three

8
00:00:17.924 --> 00:00:19.880
slots XOR to its fingerprint.

9
00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.310
A lookup checks that equation.

10
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.060
A stored key always passes.

11
00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.679
An absent key passes by chance

12
00:00:27.679 --> 00:00:30.241
one time in two hundred fifty-six

13
00:00:30.241 --> 00:00:32.290
for eight-bit fingerprints.

14
00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.724
Each key is an edge joining three slots

15
00:00:35.724 --> 00:00:38.150
the keys form a three-hypergraph.

16
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.648
Peeling solves one XOR equation per key

17
00:00:41.648 --> 00:00:43.300
without any algebra.

18
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.370
Here are eight keys in twelve slots.

19
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.747
For every slot, count how many keys use it

20
00:00:49.747 --> 00:00:52.000
and keep the XOR of those keys.

21
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.380
Slots one

22
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.500
two and eleven each have a single key.

23
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.396
Such a slot belongs to that key alone

24
00:00:59.396 --> 00:01:02.210
and the XOR kept there is the key itself.

25
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.720
Remove the key from its three slots

26
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.120
and remember the key with its own slot.

27
00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:10.830
Key sixty-six goes first, from slot eleven.

28
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.055
That leaves slot five with a single key

29
00:01:14.055 --> 00:01:16.140
twelve, so twelve goes next.

30
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:17.290
Repeat.

31
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.880
Here all eight keys come off.

32
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.606
Now fill the table

33
00:01:21.666 --> 00:01:23.295
going back through the keys

34
00:01:23.355 --> 00:01:24.380
in reverse order.

35
00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.879
Each key's own slot is still free

36
00:01:26.942 --> 00:01:28.649
so set it to whatever makes

37
00:01:28.712 --> 00:01:29.850
its equation hold.

38
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.480
Why does this work?

39
00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.643
Keys peeled later never use

40
00:01:33.711 --> 00:01:35.457
an earlier key's own slot

41
00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:37.270
and they are filled first.

42
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.318
So each key's other two slots are already

43
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:42.423
final when its own slot is set

44
00:01:42.423 --> 00:01:44.660
and nothing that holds is broken.

45
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.200
Peeling needs a slot with one key to start.

46
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.017
With too few slots, it gets stuck

47
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.266
every remaining slot is shared

48
00:01:53.339 --> 00:01:54.790
by two or more keys.

49
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.939
For random three-hypergraphs

50
00:01:57.001 --> 00:01:59.173
there is a sharp threshold near one

51
00:01:59.235 --> 00:02:01.220
point two two two slots per key.

52
00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.168
With two thousand keys and ten seeds

53
00:02:04.168 --> 00:02:06.837
at one point one eight slots per key

54
00:02:06.837 --> 00:02:07.890
no seed peels.

55
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.360
At one point two one, one seed does.

56
00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.159
At one point two four, eight do

57
00:02:14.159 --> 00:02:16.990
and at one point two seven, all ten.

58
00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.864
Pause and predict.

59
00:02:18.864 --> 00:02:20.374
Below the threshold

60
00:02:20.374 --> 00:02:22.530
would trying more seeds help?

61
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:26.374
No.

62
00:02:26.374 --> 00:02:28.869
At two thousand keys the threshold

63
00:02:28.943 --> 00:02:30.191
is still blurred

64
00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:33.200
but with millions of keys it is a cliff.

65
00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.735
So an xor filter uses one point

66
00:02:35.805 --> 00:02:37.497
two three slots per key

67
00:02:37.497 --> 00:02:40.034
nine point eight four bits per key

68
00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:42.430
for one in two hundred fifty-six.

69
00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:44.470
To sum up.

70
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.842
Every key's three slots XOR

71
00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:48.340
to its fingerprint.

72
00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.076
Peel the slots used by one key

73
00:02:51.076 --> 00:02:52.770
then fill in reverse.

74
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.214
And peeling needs about one point

75
00:02:55.277 --> 00:02:56.800
two three slots per key.

76
00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.183
As an exercise, peel the eight keys by hand

77
00:03:00.183 --> 00:03:01.630
and check the table.

78
00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.064
Next, the binary fuse filter

79
00:03:04.064 --> 00:03:06.220
which peels with fewer slots.
